window.AG_PRACTICE["baduanjin"] = {"id":"baduanjin","axis":"daoyin","seal":"導","scene":"art/scene-garden.jpg","title":"Eight Brocades","titleZh":"八段錦","sub":"Eight movements, eight intentions — learn what each one is for","subZh":"八式八意——明其所為而後行","mentor":{"key":"xiong","nm":"Xióng Shī","nmZh":"熊師","line":"\"Anyone can copy the shapes. The shapes are the easy part. Each movement was given a line of verse because the line tells you what it is FOR — and knowing that changes how you do it.\"","lineZh":"「動作誰都學得會，那是最容易的部分。每一式配一句訣，是因為那句話說出它為什麼要這樣做——知道了，做法就不一樣了。」"},"card":{"h":"Eight Lines, Eight Intentions 八訣八意","hZh":"","p":"Each of the eight brocades carries a line of verse naming its purpose: regulating the San Jiao, opening the chest, harmonising the Spleen and Stomach, releasing strain, cooling the Heart, strengthening the low back and Kidney, building strength, and shaking out the residue.","pZh":"八式各繫一訣，訣即其意：理三焦、開胸、調脾胃、去五勞、去心火、固腎腰、增氣力、消百病。"},"note":"Educational movement content. Move within a comfortable range, stop if anything hurts, and check with a clinician first if you are pregnant, recovering from injury or surgery, or managing a health condition. 本組為運動教學。動作宜在舒適範圍內，若有疼痛即止。懷孕、傷後、術後或有慢性疾病者，宜先諮詢醫療人員。","items":[{"k":"order","q":"Put the first four brocades in their traditional order.","qZh":"排出八段錦前四式。","seq":[{"t":"Two hands hold up the heavens","c":"雙手托天理三焦"},{"t":"Drawing the bow to each side","c":"左右開弓似射鵰"},{"t":"Raising one hand to regulate the Spleen and Stomach","c":"調理脾胃須單舉"},{"t":"Looking back to relieve the five strains","c":"五勞七傷往後瞧"}],"note":"The order is not arbitrary. It opens the whole trunk first, then the chest and shoulders, then the middle burner, then the neck and upper back — moving from large to specific. 其序非偶然：先開通軀幹，次及胸肩，再及中焦，末及頸背，由大而細。"},{"k":"order","q":"Now the last four.","qZh":"再排後四式。","seq":[{"t":"Swaying the head and shaking the tail","c":"搖頭擺尾去心火"},{"t":"Two hands climb the feet","c":"兩手攀足固腎腰"},{"t":"Clenching the fists with fierce eyes","c":"攢拳怒目增氣力"},{"t":"Bouncing on the heels seven times","c":"背後七顛百病消"}],"note":"The set closes with the heel bounce — a small movement that settles everything the previous seven stirred up. Sets in this tradition almost always end with something gathering rather than something expanding. 末以七顛收之：其動雖小，所以收前七式之所動。導引之套，多以收束為終，不以發散為終。"},{"k":"mc","q":"\"Two hands hold up the heavens\" is said to regulate what?","qZh":"「雙手托天」所理者何？","opts":[{"t":"The San Jiao — the passage for qi and fluids through the three burners","c":"三焦 — 氣與水之通道","ok":1,"note":"The full stretch from fingertips to heels lengthens the whole trunk at once, which is the physical version of opening a passage that runs top to bottom. It is placed first for exactly that reason. 自指端至足跟之全身伸展，通貫上下，正合三焦上下通行之義。故列為首式。"},{"t":"The Liver","c":"肝","ok":0,"note":"The Liver is addressed more in the side-bending and eye-related work. 肝之調多在側屈與目之運用。"},{"t":"The Heart alone","c":"獨心","ok":0,"note":"The Heart is the fifth brocade's business. 心屬第五式所主。"},{"t":"The Kidney alone","c":"獨腎","ok":0,"note":"The Kidney is the sixth brocade's business. 腎屬第六式所主。"}]},{"k":"match","q":"Match each brocade to what its verse says it is for.","qZh":"八段錦各式配其訣意。","pairs":[{"l":"Drawing the bow 左右開弓","lZh":"","r":"Opens the chest and shoulders","rZh":"開胸展肩"},{"l":"Raising one hand 調理脾胃單舉","lZh":"","r":"Harmonises the Spleen and Stomach","rZh":"調理脾胃"},{"l":"Looking back 五勞七傷往後瞧","lZh":"","r":"Releases strain in the neck and upper back","rZh":"去五勞七傷"},{"l":"Two hands climb the feet 兩手攀足","lZh":"","r":"Strengthens the low back and Kidney","rZh":"固腎腰"},{"l":"Heel bounce 背後七顛","lZh":"","r":"Settles and disperses residue","rZh":"消百病，收束全套"}],"note":"Learning the verse is learning the intention. A student who knows that the fourth brocade is for strain will turn slowly and far; a student who does not will whip the head round and gain nothing. 記其訣即記其意。知第四式為去勞者，其轉必緩而深；不知者，掉首而已，無所得也。"},{"k":"mc","q":"\"Swaying the head and shaking the tail\" is traditionally said to do what?","qZh":"「搖頭擺尾」，傳統謂其何用？","opts":[{"t":"Draw down Heart fire","c":"去心火","ok":1,"note":"The image is heat at the top being drawn downward by a low, rooted movement in the hips and legs. In practice it is the most demanding brocade on the thighs, which is part of the mechanism — attention and effort move downward, and the head quietens. 上有火而以下盤沉穩之動引之下行。此式最耗股力，其理亦在此：心力向下，其上乃靜。"},{"t":"Warm the Kidney yang","c":"溫腎陽","ok":0,"note":"Warming the low back is the sixth brocade. 溫腰者第六式。"},{"t":"Open the Lung","c":"宣肺","ok":0,"note":"Chest opening is the second brocade. 開胸者第二式。"},{"t":"Move Liver qi","c":"疏肝","ok":0,"note":"Not the stated intention of this one. 非本式訣中之意。"}]},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the most common mistake beginners make in Eight Brocades?","qZh":"初學八段錦，最常之誤為何？","opts":[{"t":"Moving fast and holding the breath","c":"動之過速，且屏其息","ok":1,"note":"The set is slow on purpose: the breath leads and the movement follows, so speed destroys the point of it. Breathe out on the effort, in on the return, and never hold. Slow also protects the joints, which matters more with age. 此套本主緩：息為之先，形隨其後，速則盡失其義。用力時呼，還原時吸，不可閉氣。緩亦護關節，年長者尤宜。"},{"t":"Doing all eight in order","c":"依序行完八式","ok":0,"note":"That is the correct practice. 此正其常法。"},{"t":"Standing with the feet apart","c":"開步而立","ok":0,"note":"A stable stance is correct — usually about shoulder width. 立宜穩，多與肩同寬。"},{"t":"Repeating a movement several times","c":"一式重複數遍","ok":0,"note":"Repetition is normal; three to eight repetitions per movement is common. 重複為常，每式三至八遍。"}]},{"k":"mc","q":"How does Eight Brocades differ from Five Animal Frolics 五禽戲?","qZh":"八段錦與五禽戲何異？","opts":[{"t":"Eight Brocades is a fixed sequence of eight; Five Animal Frolics imitates five animals, each with its own quality","c":"八段錦八式定序；五禽戲象五禽，各具其性","ok":1,"note":"Both are daoyin 導引 — guided movement with breath and attention. Eight Brocades is compact and standardised, easy to do daily in a small space. Five Animal Frolics, attributed to Hua Tuo, works through the character of tiger, deer, bear, ape and crane, and is looser and more expressive. 二者皆導引：形、息、意三者相合。八段錦簡而定，日行於方寸之地即可；五禽戲相傳出於華佗，以虎、鹿、熊、猿、鳥五者之性而運，較為舒放。"},{"t":"Eight Brocades is only for older people","c":"八段錦專為年長者設","ok":0,"note":"It is used across all ages, including by athletes as a warm-up. 各年齡皆用之，運動員亦以之為熱身。"},{"t":"Five Animal Frolics has no breathing element","c":"五禽戲不涉呼吸","ok":0,"note":"Breath is central to both. 二者皆以息為要。"},{"t":"They are the same set with different names","c":"二者名異而實同","ok":0,"note":"Different sets, different origins, different forms. 套路、來源、形制皆不同。"}]},{"k":"mc","q":"Which safety point belongs with teaching this set?","qZh":"教授此套，何項安全提醒為必要？","opts":[{"t":"Work within a comfortable range, stop if anything hurts, and check first if pregnant, recovering, or managing a condition","c":"動在舒適之度，痛則即止；孕、傷後、有疾者宜先諮詢","ok":1,"note":"The forward fold in the sixth brocade and the deep stance in the fifth are the two most commonly overdone. Range comes with practice; forcing it produces exactly the strain the set is meant to release. 第六式之前俯與第五式之下蹲，最易過度。幅度隨功而增；強求則反生其所欲去之勞。"},{"t":"Practise only at dawn","c":"惟晨間可練","ok":0,"note":"Dawn is traditional and pleasant, but any time works. Consistency matters more than the hour. 晨練為傳統且宜人，然何時皆可。貴在有恆，不在其時。"},{"t":"Never practise indoors","c":"不可於室內練","ok":0,"note":"Indoors is fine — this set was designed to need very little space. 室內可也。此套本以省地為長。"},{"t":"Always practise on an empty stomach","c":"必空腹而練","ok":0,"note":"Avoiding a heavy meal immediately before is sensible; requiring an empty stomach is not. 飽食即動者不宜，然不必空腹。"}]},{"k":"mc","q":"Why does the set end with bouncing on the heels seven times?","qZh":"何以以「背後七顛」收束全套？","opts":[{"t":"To settle what the previous movements stirred, and close the practice","c":"收前式之所動，以結其功","ok":1,"note":"Every daoyin set ends by gathering rather than expanding — you do not leave a practice in a stirred-up state. The small repeated drop also gives a gentle jolt through the spine and legs, which is why it is described as shaking out what is left. 導引諸套，皆以收而不以散為終，不可留其動而去。輕落數次，其震自脊而下，故訣言「消百病」。"},{"t":"To raise the heart rate at the end","c":"以升其心率","ok":0,"note":"The opposite — the closing movement calms. 適反。收式主靜。"},{"t":"Because seven is a lucky number","c":"以七為吉數","ok":0,"note":"Seven is conventional, but the function of the closing movement is the real reason it is there. 七為成數之習，然其所以在此者，為其收束之功。"},{"t":"To stretch the calves","c":"以伸小腿","ok":0,"note":"The calves do work, but that is incidental to the closing function. 小腿雖有所用，然非其主義。"}]},{"k":"mc","q":"What does 導引 daoyin actually mean?","qZh":"「導引」二字何解？","opts":[{"t":"Guiding qi and drawing the body — movement, breath and attention together","c":"導氣令和，引體令柔——形、息、意三者相合","ok":1,"note":"The classical gloss is 導氣令和，引體令柔: guide the qi so it flows evenly, draw the body so it becomes supple. Movement without breath is exercise; movement with breath and attention is daoyin. That is the whole distinction. 古注云：導氣令和，引體令柔。有形而無息者，運動而已；形、息、意俱者，乃為導引。其別在此。"},{"t":"Meditation performed sitting still","c":"靜坐而已","ok":0,"note":"Sitting meditation is a different practice; daoyin moves. 靜坐別為一法；導引在動。"},{"t":"Massage performed by a practitioner","c":"醫者所施之推拿","ok":0,"note":"That is tui na 推拿. Daoyin is self-practice. 彼為推拿。導引者自為之。"},{"t":"Breathing exercises only","c":"專指呼吸之法","ok":0,"note":"Breath alone is closer to tu na 吐納. Daoyin combines breath with movement. 專於息者近於吐納。導引則形息並行。"}]}]};
